Phase 1: the pixel matrix and the transforms that survive the grid

What it adds: a plain picture structure the core layer can reason about, and
the three transforms that land exactly on the pixel grid - whole-pixel
translation, mirroring about a vertical or horizontal line, and a lean built
from whole-row displacements.

Why it was needed: the core produced positions and nothing else. Everything
about what was drawn lived on the far side of a boundary this layer may not
cross, so none of it could be tested without the editor. The matrix is the
agreement that moves that work back to where it can be.

The rule that runs through all of it: no transform may invent a colour. It is
enforced, not asserted - the tests apply a shared check that the colours
coming out are a subset of the colours going in. A blended value is a colour
the artist never chose and, on an indexed sprite, one that cannot be
represented at all.

Decisions worth keeping: coordinates start at zero to match the application's
own images, since two conventions in one pipeline is how off-by-one happens;
reading past the edge answers 'nothing drawn' rather than failing, because a
transform sampling its source legitimately asks; writing past the edge is
dropped rather than growing the picture; ties when rounding a displacement go
the same way every run, so the same input always gives the same picture.

The lean is deliberately not an affine shear. A real shear samples between
pixels and blurs; this slides whole rows, so neighbouring rows share a
displacement and the result is the staircase a pixel artist would draw.

Gates: 121 unit tests pass, linter and formatter clean across 38 files, core
layer clean, core coverage 100 percent across 9 files.
